Here's what I found about using @bomb.sh/tab with Commander.js:

## Overview

Tab provides shell autocompletions for JavaScript CLIs, and if you already use Commander.js, Tab can read your existing CLI definitions through adapters and generate completions automatically.

## Basic Usage Example

To use tab with Commander.js, you import the Command class from 'commander' and the tab adapter from '@bomb.sh/tab/commander'. You define your commands and options normally, then initialize tab completions by passing your program instance to the tab function.

Here's a concrete example from the README:

```javascript
import { Command } from 'commander';
import tab from '@bomb.sh/tab/commander';

const program = new Command('my-cli');
program.version('1.0.0');

// Define commands
program
  .command('serve')
  .description('Start the server')
  .option('-p, --port <number>', 'port to use', '3000')
  .option('-H, --host <host>', 'host to use', 'localhost')
  .action((options) => {
    console.log('Starting server...');
  });

// Initialize tab completions
const completion = tab(program);

// Add custom completions
const serveCommand = completion.commands.get('serve');
const portOption = serveCommand?.options.get('port');
if (portOption) {
  portOption.handler = (complete) => {
    complete('3000', 'Default port');
    complete('8080', 'Alternative port');
  };
}

program.parse();
```

The Commander integration supports customizing the command name used to generate shell completion scripts—the default is "complete," but you can use a custom name.

For more detailed information, check the [official Tab documentation](https://bomb.sh/docs/tab/) and the [GitHub repository](https://github.com/bombshell-dev/tab).


